    "chunk_size_bytes": 1355,
    "chunk_text": "Monetary Fund may be having their strictures and wanting us to increase our revenue; they are looking at these issues in a different perspective. But in negotiating with them, we must consider whether we have done justice to the investment climate in this country.\n\nNow, I have before me a statement by Mr. Arjuna Herath, the Chairman of the Board of Investment published in the \"Daily Mirror\". He says, I quote:\n\n\u201cIMF acts as biggest impediment in drawing Foreign Direct Investments to Sri Lanka as policies render country unattractive.\u201d\n\nThis is their own, current Chairman of the Board of Investment. He is tendering his resignation, he says. The \"Daily Mirror\" further reports, I quote:\n\n\"Mr. Arjuna Herath, the Chairman of Board of Investment, the one stop shop for attracting investments in December announced that he would step down from his post by the end of this month, just under 16 months into his tenure.\n\nAny tax incentive that the BOI wants to offer beyond what is currently there needs the IMF\u2019s rubber stamp....\"\n\n\"Sri Lanka does not offer any compelling reason for global industrialists or investors to relocate to Sri Lanka with its current tax policy and challenges.\u201d\n\nThe situation being such, your own Chairman of the Board of Investment is saying this about the IMF. So, what type of negotiations have you done with the IMF?"
  },
