    "chunk_size_bytes": 1534,
    "chunk_text": "\"Sri Lanka does not offer any compelling reason for global industrialists or investors to relocate to Sri Lanka with its current tax policy and challenges.\u201d\n\nThe situation being such, your own Chairman of the Board of Investment is saying this about the IMF. So, what type of negotiations have you done with the IMF?\n\n335 336\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db1\u0dd3\u0dd2\u0da5 \u0dbb\u0dc0\u0dd4\u0dc6\u0dbb \u0dc4\u0d9a\u0dd3 \u0da2 \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dc0 ]\n\n2026 \u0dad\u0dcf\u0dc0 \u0dbb\u0dd2 07\n\nEven the secondary investments that are coming should have that particular provision for employees. If employees are to pay a 36 per cent Income Tax, who will relocate here? That is the issue. So, the incentives we have now revised stand at 7.5 per cent for IT industries. If an IT firm relocates here and wants to export their products abroad, they will be charged 7.5 per cent, whereas outside this special economic zone, they will be charged 15 per cent. That is why they have said that because it is 15 per cent, we will make it more rational and bring it down to seven-and-a-half per cent, whereas there are other economic zones elsewhere in the country which charge only zero per cent tax. In those, there is no charge at all, no Income Tax at all. So, who will come and relocate here? That is the whole problem. So, as far as the IMF is concerned, we should have had much more robust negotiations with them to force their hand to see that the existing concession should continue. But, now we have revised it and brought it to 7.5 per cent, which, again, is uncompetitive when you compare with other zones."
  },
