    "chunk_text": "Even the secondary investments that are coming should have that particular provision for employees. If employees are to pay a 36 per cent Income Tax, who will relocate here? That is the issue. So, the incentives we have now revised stand at 7.5 per cent for IT industries. If an IT firm relocates here and wants to export their products abroad, they will be charged 7.5 per cent, whereas outside this special economic zone, they will be charged 15 per cent. That is why they have said that because it is 15 per cent, we will make it more rational and bring it down to seven-and-a-half per cent, whereas there are other economic zones elsewhere in the country which charge only zero per cent tax. In those, there is no charge at all, no Income Tax at all. So, who will come and relocate here? That is the whole problem. So, as far as the IMF is concerned, we should have had much more robust negotiations with them to force their hand to see that the existing concession should continue. But, now we have revised it and brought it to 7.5 per cent, which, again, is uncompetitive when you compare with other zones.\n\nSo, a small pittance is going to come in. If they look at the value-added advantage of this development, they are expecting almost another 13 billion dollars, if all the\n\ninvestments do become a reality. That, again, is a moot point. In fact, the Chairman of the Committee advised that they speak to the Department of Census and Statistics and see whether, realistically, the value addition to the economy would be in the range of 13 billion dollars. Our entire economy is about 100 billion dollars. So, that is about 13 per cent of our GDP! That is a massive change and it would be welcome to the economy, if that happens. But then, with this kind of incentives offered, having the incentive we had originally given revised or downsized, will there be investor confidence in the country\u2019s infrastructure sector, especially in this economic zone we have set up?"
