  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-01-07-2026-01-07",
    "num": "2026-01-07",
    "date_str": "2026-01-07",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-01-07",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23112_english_2026-01-07.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-01-07-2026-01-07-0201",
    "chunk_index": 201,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "f13f3898b8f8294c817b912cbc0e1103",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1335,
    "chunk_text": "The Minister is not in the House and Hon. Deputy Minister, I would like to make some constructive criticism with regard to this Bill. I would like to say with responsibility that the proposed Amendments create systematic risks, which could destabilize the domestic economy. Sir, the Amendment to Section 42(1) states that\n\nPrior to the Amendment to Section 42, the Banking Act operated, except for Part IV. Now, with the Amendment, the Central Bank has to make regulations in terms of the international banking standards. In terms of the Banking Act, international banking standards are made practicable as far as possible. For example, the capital adequacy ratio in terms of the Basel III is eight per cent. However, the Central Bank issued a directive that it should be 10 per cent, thereby protecting our domestic banking system.\n\nWith the present Amendment, whatever directives issued would be in terms of the international banking standards. International banking standards are very fluid and there is no proper definition. So, there is greater ambiguity. Therefore, I propose that an amendment be made to make the international banking standards practicable as far as possible, thereby giving the Governing Board of the Central Bank to take decisions in the interest of our banking system with regard to minimum capital adequacy."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-01-07-2026-01-07",
    "num": "2026-01-07",
