    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "4602df26970d7002a682be907f6f5cff",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1696,
    "chunk_text": "reflects the structural characteristics of a developing and a small open economy, which faces greater exposure to external shocks, supply-side disruptions and exchange rate volatility. In such a context, aiming for a very low inflation target would be excessively costly in terms of output and employment.\n\nbetween 2009 to 2021, inflation averaged around 5 per cent in Sri Lanka and from 1978 to 2008, the average inflation was 12 per cent per annum.\n\nFurther, quantitative analysis using Sri Lanka\u2019s historical data revealed that maintaining the inflation target around 5 per cent helps maximize economic growth, to which the statistical models are used to gauge these average calculations.\n\n\uf0b7 From a policy perspective, targeting an inflation rate as low as 2 per cent at initial stages of inflation targeting would necessitate maintaining persistently tight monetary conditions, which could suppress investment, slow down economic recovery and raise unemployment, outcomes that are undesirable for an emerging market economy. Therefore, the 5 per cent target strikes a balanced approach under the Flexible Inflation Targeting - FIT - framework. This allows sufficient flexibility to accommodate temporary shocks while anchoring inflation expectations at a credible and realistic level. For the same reason, most of the emerging market and developing economies, in general, set inflation targets above 2 per cent.\n\n2. \uf0b7 Monetary policy decisions in most countries are made on a broader perspective, considering the overall macroeconomic conditions to maintain price stability, rather than only considering the interest cost to the Government, which is only one stakeholder of the economy."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-01-08-2026-01-08",
    "num": "2026-01-08",
    "date_str": "2026-01-08",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-01-08",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23118_english_2026-01-08.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-01-08-2026-01-08-0061",
    "chunk_index": 61,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "aef1bef985cd1400a012acc842c8d509",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1857,
    "chunk_text": "2. \uf0b7 Monetary policy decisions in most countries are made on a broader perspective, considering the overall macroeconomic conditions to maintain price stability, rather than only considering the interest cost to the Government, which is only one stakeholder of the economy.\n\n3. \uf0b7 Under the FIT framework, the Central Bank is responsible for formulating and implementing monetary policy while managing a flexible exchange rate regime aimed at achieving and maintaining domestic price stability. In this framework, the exchange rate is determined largely by market supply and demand conditions with\n\n485 486\n\n[\u0dd0\u0dbb\u0dd4 (\u0d86\u0da0\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0da2) \u0d85\u0dd2\u0dba\u0dbd\u0dca \u0dca\u200d\u0da2\u0dca\u0dca\u200d \u0dad\u0db3\u0dca\u200d\u0dcf]\n\n2026 \u0dca\u200d\u0db1\u0dc0\u0dcf\u0dd2\u0db4 08\n\nlimited intervention by the Central Bank solely to smooth excessive short-term volatility. Accordingly, the exchange rate acts as a shock absorber under this arrangement, thus helping to avoid building vulnerabilities in the economy. Historically, attempts to maintain exchange rate stability beyond the economic fundamentals have proven to be unsuccessful.\n\n\uf0b7 The 1 per cent depreciation has both positive and negative impacts on different stakeholders. For example, there are benefits to exporters, remittances and the tourism industry, whereas a negative impact on importers.\n\n\uf0b7 Maintaining and achieving inflation around 5 per cent helps anchor expectations and supports currency stability by promoting confidence in the domestic monetary framework. A credible and consistent inflation target reduces speculative pressures, supports orderly exchange rate movements and strengthens macroeconomic and external stability in the long term.\n\n4. \uf0b7 The medium-term inflation target of 5 per cent is expected to ensure overall price stability, which is essential for sustaining economic growth and maintaining public confidence."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-01-08-2026-01-08",
    "num": "2026-01-08",
    "date_str": "2026-01-08",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-01-08",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23118_english_2026-01-08.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-01-08-2026-01-08-0062",
    "chunk_index": 62,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "92cb34578396bbe861abf341eea02971",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1930,
    "chunk_text": "\uf0b7 Maintaining and achieving inflation around 5 per cent helps anchor expectations and supports currency stability by promoting confidence in the domestic monetary framework. A credible and consistent inflation target reduces speculative pressures, supports orderly exchange rate movements and strengthens macroeconomic and external stability in the long term.\n\n4. \uf0b7 The medium-term inflation target of 5 per cent is expected to ensure overall price stability, which is essential for sustaining economic growth and maintaining public confidence.\n\n\uf0b7 The target of 5 per cent is set with several considerations such as the historical inflation trends and all those things are also basic macroeconomic factors.\n\n\uf0b7 The inflation objectives, therefore, seek to introduce price pressures, enabling the real growth led by productivity, competitiveness and value addition, rather than nominal gains arising from inflationary pressure.\n\n\uf0b7 The Central Bank also supports the enhancement of real economic growth, supported by structural reforms and productivity improvements.\n\n5. \uf0b7 Following the peak level of inflation of 69.8 per cent in September, 2022, inflation has been brought down to low levels through appropriate policy actions. Inflation is not maintained above 5 per cent.\n\n\uf0b7 In terms of domestic savings, maintaining inflation at a stable and predictable level helps protect the real value of savings by\n\npreventing a rapid erosion of purchasing power in deposits and fixed-income instruments. Stable inflation also helps keep real interest rates steady, which, in turn, encourages long-term savings and financial planning.\n\n\uf0b7 In this regard, a well-defined and credible inflation target helps reduce the erosion of domestic savings by serving as an anchor for price stability. When the target is clearly communicated and consistently pursued, it helps shape public and market expectations about future inflation."
  },
