    "md5": "2a9534d3ff0c71ae31fc8d7c36551782",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1901,
    "chunk_text": "The Government of the United Kingdom has imposed unilateral travel bans and asset freezes on several Sri Lankan nationals, including former senior military officers, outside any UN Security Council mandate, international judicial determination or bilateral legal process. The Government of Sri Lanka itself described these actions as unilateral, and appointed a ministerial committee to examine the matter. Yet, no clear Cabinet decision, official policy position or a disclosure to Parliament has followed in, creating uncertainty, domestically and internationally.\n\nThis issue transcends individual cases; it concerns sovereign equality, due process, non-interference and Parliament's constitutional role in oversight of foreign policy.\n\n1. Does the Government affirm that Sri Lanka is a plural and democratic society, in which Sinhalese, Tamils, Muslims and all other communities coexist, following the defeat of terrorism by the armed forces and the Police?\n\n2. Has the Cabinet-appointed ministerial committee submitted its report, on what date were its recommendations placed before Cabinet, what decisions, if any, were taken and why had they not been formally communicated to Parliament? 3. What legal, diplomatic or multilateral options were considered, including recourse to international law or international forums, and which were accepted or rejected, with reasons? 4. Did the Cabinet assess the implications of these unilateral measures on Sri Lanka's sovereignty, civil-military relations and institutional integrity and what conclusions were reached? 5. What representations have been made to the UK Government since the imposition of these sanctions and what outcomes, if any, have resulted? 6. Has the Government evaluated the precedent this sets for the States imposing extra-territorial measures against Sri Lankan nationals without due process and what safeguards are now in place?"
  },
