    "chunk_size_bytes": 1907,
    "chunk_text": "01. During the year 2025, the process of handing over of issuance of Treasury Bills and Treasury Bonds from the Central Bank of Sri Lanka to the Ministry of Finance was commenced in accordance with the Finance Act and the PDMO Act. Therefore, this issue would be addressed, upon the completion of the takeover process, from 01st January, 2026.\n\na. The investment composition of the Employees\u2019 Provident Fund - EPF - is primarily driven by available domestic market opportunities. Given the substantial size of the fund, market opportunities remain limited. As a superannuation fund, investment in fixed-income securities is prioritized to ensure consistent, long-term returns. Government securities are considered as the lowest-risk investment instrument and the most practical major investment option, given the size of the Fund. b. The details of returns paid by EPF on member balances prevailed at end of each year from 2020-2024 after paying 14 per cent Income Tax, with the comparison of other market indicators, are given in the table below.\n\nInstrument Book Value\n\n(Rs \u2018000) Composition\n\nGovernment Securities 4,616,977,889 94.34 %\n\nCorporate Debt Instruments 22,135,467 0.45 %\n\nListed Equities 191,160,756 3.91 %\n\nUnlisted Equities 45,171,692 0.92% Other 18,650,000 0.38%\n\nTotal 4,894,095,804\n\nYear\n\nRate of Return on EPF Member Balances (%)\n\nRate of Return offered by Employees Trust Fund (%)\n\n1 Year Fixed Deposits rate of a Major Savings Bank (%)\n\nCommercial Banks\u2019 Average Weighted Deposits Rate (AWDR) (%)\n\nAnnual Average Inflation CCPI (2021=100) (%)\n\n2020 9.00 8.00 5.25 5.80 4.60\n\n2021 9.00 7.50 5.50 4.94 6.00\n\n2022 9.00 8.75 12.00 14.06 46.40\n\n2023 13.00 10.00 8.00 11.64 17.40\n\n2024 11.00 10.00 7.00 7.53 1.20\n\nIn all the years, except in the year 2022, the yield or the\n\nrate of return given by the EPF to its members had been higher.\n\n04. The Answer given in No. 02 above applies."
